\begin_layout Section
Gate Level Simulations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Post-synthesis Gate-Level Simulation (GLS) is a crucial step in the ASIC
 design flow that verifies the logical correctness of the synthesized netlist
 under timing constraints.
 Unlike functional simulation, which operates on RTL and assumes ideal condition
s with zero delays, gate-level simulation uses the synthesized netlist and
 includes real-world timing information derived from the synthesis process,
 often via SDF (Standard Delay Format) annotation.
 This allows the simulation to reflect actual propagation delays, setup/hold
 timings, and logic optimizations performed during synthesis.
 GLS helps catch issues like race conditions, hold/setup violations, and
 uninitialized registers that are typically not visible in functional simulation.
 However, gate-level simulations are significantly slower due to the inclusion
 of detailed timing and structural netlist elements.
 Common challenges faced during GLS include mismatches due to uninitialized
 flip-flops (especially if they were not properly reset in RTL), glitches
 caused by combinational paths, and difficulty debugging timing-related
 issues due to the lack of high-level signal abstraction.
 Despite these challenges, GLS is essential for ensuring that the design
 will operate reliably when implemented in silicon.
